course: dictionary
lesson: 14279
title: Nonstop
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Nonstop means without stopping or pausing. «The train ran nonstop from Leeds to London.» The train did not stop on the way, so nonstop tells you it kept going all the way.
explain: Nonstop can also mean something that happens all the time. «It rained nonstop all weekend.» The rain did not take a break. The clue is «all weekend», which tells you the rain went on for ages.
explain: Look at the words near nonstop to decide which meaning fits. Then try your meaning in the sentence. If "without stopping" still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Nonstop means without stopping or pausing.
fact: A nonstop flight, train or bus goes straight to its end without stopping on the way.
fact: Informally, nonstop can mean happening all the time, so much that it feels like it never ends.
fact: «Nonstop» can be an adjective, as in «a nonstop flight», or an adverb, as in «she talks nonstop».
fact: It is also spelled «non-stop».
exercise: «We took a nonstop flight to Spain, so we did not land anywhere on the way.» What does nonstop mean here? | Full of food | Happening only once | *Without stopping on the way | Very slow and tired
exercise: «Mum said the baby cried nonstop all night.» What does nonstop suggest? | It stopped at the station | *It went on all the time, without a break | It was quiet and calm | It happened once at midnight
exercise: Sam cycled nonstop for 3 hours in a maths problem. Did Sam stop for breaks? | Yes, he stopped every hour | Yes, he stopped for a rest after one hour | He never started cycling | *No, he kept going for the whole 3 hours
test: «The bus went nonstop from the city to the coast.» What does nonstop mean? | Full of people | Late as always | *Without stopping at any stop on the way | Very fast and dangerous
test: «My brother can talk nonstop about football.» What does nonstop mean here? | Quietly and softly | Badly | *All the time, without pausing | Only once
exam: Which sentence uses nonstop to mean without stopping on a journey? | «He ate nonstop all day.» | «She was nonstop happy about it.» | *«The train ran nonstop from Bristol to Edinburgh.» | «The children played nonstop in the park.»
